# Force LF on everything that is executed rather than merely read.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites checked-out text
# files to CRLF. A shell script with CRLF endings fails before it runs a line —
# bash reads the shebang as "/usr/bin/env bash\r" and reports "bad interpreter",
# or trips over "\r: command not found". The same applies inside the Docker
# images, whose base layers are Linux regardless of the host.
#
# These rules make the ending part of the file's identity, so a Windows clone
# gets LF no matter how the user has configured autocrlf.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
